00:05:33.883 --> 00:05:42.226
This first news piece that I have brought today is about pterosaurs, because there is not enough pterosaur talk on the Common Descent podcast.

00:05:42.647 --> 00:05:43.147
This is true.

00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:48.309
Pterosaurs are, of course, the famous flying reptiles from the age of dinosaurs.

00:05:49.543 --> 00:06:01.567
Pterosaurs lasted throughout the entirety of the Jurassic and Cretaceous periods, and before that, they disappeared during the mass extinction at the end of the Cretaceous, along with most of the other dinosaurs.

00:06:02.107 --> 00:06:14.072
But an interesting thing that has come up in people's research of pterosaurs is that there seem to be less pterosaurs, lower diversity right up toward the end of the Cretaceous,

00:06:14.429 --> 00:06:24.913
which has left some people wondering if the pterosaurs were struggling for a while leading up to the mass extinction, if they were sort of on their way out in some ways.

00:06:25.393 --> 00:06:36.356
However, a new study has reported on a new fossil site that has shown us that pterosaurs at the very end of the Cretaceous were actually doing quite a bit better than we thought they were.

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:36.917
Cool.

00:06:37.165 --> 00:06:40.506
This is a study by Nick Longrich et al. in PLOS Biology.

00:06:41.026 --> 00:06:45.708
These new discoveries come from a couple of phosphate mines in Morocco.

00:06:45.788 --> 00:06:49.189
This is the late Maastrichtian, which is the very end of the Cretaceous.

00:06:50.569 --> 00:06:58.552
Before this, all the pterosaurs known from the end of the Cretaceous are one family of pterosaurs, known as the Agedarchids.

00:06:59.105 --> 00:07:12.990
But in this fossil deposit, they found over 200 fossil specimens, representing six new species and one already known species, in three different families.

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:20.572
There are Agedarchid pterosaurs, Pteranodontid pterosaur remains, and Nyctosaurid remains,

00:07:21.588 --> 00:07:30.791
which is really cool because it means that there was a much higher diversity of pterosaurs right up at the end than our other fossil remains have indicated.

00:07:31.431 --> 00:07:43.114
This is not only the largest collection of pterosaurs from this time period, and the pterosaurs themselves range from wingspans generally three to six meters.

00:07:44.028 --> 00:07:46.389
So we're looking at 10 to 20 foot wingspans.

00:07:47.029 --> 00:07:58.552
There are remains at the site. One of the species that they found appears to have been one of the giant pterosaurs, with wingspans up to perhaps nine meters.

00:07:58.732 --> 00:08:01.553
Wow. Yeah, no, huge.

00:08:02.366 --> 00:08:10.790
So not only does this suggest that there was more of a diversity, right, we've got multiple families of pterosaurs surviving right up to the end of the Cretaceous,

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:20.553
which is to say that they weren't in decline, or at least certainly not as much as people had suggested, but they also looked at what's called functional diversity.

00:08:21.468 --> 00:08:31.731
So when you're looking at functional diversity, you're basically saying, how many, let's look at diet, feeding mode, locomotion, body size, habitat,

00:08:32.472 --> 00:08:36.613
all the sort of the niche, how many niches are these animals filling?

00:08:37.954 --> 00:08:44.276
And when they compared functional diversity at this site, other sites of this time, sites a little bit earlier,

00:08:45.105 --> 00:08:53.087
they found that functional diversity was not decreasing toward the end of the Cretaceous, and their results actually found it increasing slightly.

00:08:53.428 --> 00:08:53.988
Interesting.

00:08:54.308 --> 00:09:02.530
Which suggests that pterosaurs were not only doing quite well toward the end of the Cretaceous, but were still dominant in their ecosystems,

00:09:03.231 --> 00:09:07.432
occupying many different niches, still the biggest flying things around.

00:09:08.605 --> 00:09:20.449
So this tells us a little bit more about how pterosaurs were doing, raises the question as with all the other creatures of why they went extinct so dramatically at the very end of the Cretaceous,

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:30.193
and raises the possibility that there are other pterosaur families that survived longer than we thought they did, and we just haven't found their fossils yet.

00:09:30.673 --> 00:09:31.333
Very cool.

00:09:31.685 --> 00:09:38.527
Which is especially possible with pterosaurs because they're very delicate and they don't fossilize very well.

00:09:38.788 --> 00:09:51.392
No, they're extremely rare because of just everything working against them aren't most typically fossilized in marine environments after dying probably at sea,

00:09:51.408 --> 00:09:56.850
so it's one of those where they may also typically be living in places that don't want them to fossilize.

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:11.095
This actually ties into a principle in paleontology called the Senor Lips effect, which is that essentially fossil animals always look like they go extinct before they did.

00:10:11.475 --> 00:10:11.675
Yeah.

00:10:12.106 --> 00:10:16.048
Because the odds of the last one fossilizing are extremely low.

00:10:17.188 --> 00:10:24.031
So these authors are saying that our evidence of pterosaurs in decline might just be because we're missing the fossils.

00:10:24.606 --> 00:10:39.552
Yeah, well, it's the mentality of why you can never prove a negative is I can never find all the spots this rare turtle could be hiding to confirm that it's not in this environment,

00:10:40.153 --> 00:10:45.535
because there could always be that one that I'm just not finding because there's only one.

00:10:46.488 --> 00:10:55.091
And that becomes likely when you're trying to pinpoint the very last moment of a group's existence.

00:10:55.591 --> 00:10:55.871
Yeah.

00:10:55.911 --> 00:10:58.132
So hopefully there's more pterosaurs out there.

00:10:58.572 --> 00:10:59.913
That's very cool.

00:11:00.413 --> 00:11:09.596
And I find it interesting because it's a neat answer, but also does not give us further answer to the bigger question.

00:11:10.366 --> 00:11:14.128
So it's cool that they were doing well, but also one of those words like, well, then what were you doing?

00:11:15.748 --> 00:11:18.470
Which I always I kind of always enjoy those moments.

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:19.990
Absolutely.

00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:20.991
Very cool.

00:11:21.011 --> 00:11:28.634
Now, my first news article is a very interesting one because it deals with a unique feature in reptiles.

00:11:30.044 --> 00:11:39.448
This is a early case of the tail loss ability in an ancient group of reptiles separate from modern lizards.

00:11:40.008 --> 00:11:40.588
Ooh.

00:11:41.008 --> 00:11:53.753
Yeah, so as many of you probably already know, many lizards nowadays have the ability to either allow their tail to break or detach when attacked by a predator.

00:11:54.786 --> 00:12:02.148
And often the tail will even continue to wiggle to act as a distraction to give them a chance to get away whilst being attacked.

00:12:02.689 --> 00:12:11.632
This research deals with an ancient group of reptiles that seems to show signs of the same behavior or trait.

00:12:12.872 --> 00:12:17.234
And as I mentioned, separate from the lizards one, which is very cool.

00:12:17.687 --> 00:12:22.729
These reptiles are known as captorhinids, sometimes also known as cotylosaurs.

00:12:23.409 --> 00:12:33.353
And they were a very old, small two, fairly large. The largest ones were about two meters long. So, you know, just over six foot.

00:12:33.933 --> 00:12:34.753
Iguana sized.

00:12:34.953 --> 00:12:35.274
Yeah.

00:12:35.674 --> 00:12:40.376
So these were reptiles, very lizard like in their overall design.

00:12:40.424 --> 00:12:43.785
They had four limbs. They were pretty squat to the ground.

00:12:44.645 --> 00:12:49.768
You found them in the carboniferous up to the end of the Permian.

00:12:50.168 --> 00:12:58.071
This would be between 300, 255 million years ago. And they're extremely successful.

00:12:58.706 --> 00:13:07.149
They were globally found across Pangaea during the late Permian is really when they were showing their success.

00:13:08.209 --> 00:13:16.532
And classically, they have very triangular shaped heads with kind of a hooked snout, like a downturned front of their upper jaw.

00:13:16.852 --> 00:13:17.192
Yeah.

00:13:17.865 --> 00:13:28.309
So, you know, very recognizable reptile shape. LeBlanc et al. published in Scientific Reports 8 recently that when studying some of these specimens,

00:13:28.329 --> 00:13:38.893
they noticed in the tail vertebrae little cracks through the middle that are reminiscent of the tail loss vertebrae in modern lizards.

00:13:39.453 --> 00:13:42.475
That's sort of intentional planes of weakness.

00:13:42.686 --> 00:13:49.128
Yeah, exactly. Like a purposeful. It was like it like in a car where parts are meant to bend or break when in a wreck.

00:13:49.228 --> 00:13:54.050
It's a purposeful breaking point actually down the center of the vertebrae.

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:54.870
Yeah.

00:13:55.471 --> 00:14:05.494
So these small cracks were found by looking at more than 70 tail vertebrae from both juveniles and adults of fossil specimens.

00:14:06.046 --> 00:14:13.689
OK. They were able to confirm that these were natural features, not accidental cracks due to fossilization.

00:14:14.089 --> 00:14:20.631
Right. And they were comparable to the modern lizard vertebrae.

00:14:20.671 --> 00:14:26.634
So they it's all signs are pointing to that these reptiles also had this tail loss feature.

00:14:27.987 --> 00:14:39.852
And it would make sense as to one of the reasons why they may have become so successful if they were the so far as we can see, only reptile that had this special ability.

00:14:40.293 --> 00:14:40.593
Yeah.

00:14:41.426 --> 00:14:53.570
And is even more interesting to the fact that this means that this trait was evolved and then lost and then re-evolved by lizards 70 million years ago.

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:54.710
So way after.

00:14:55.050 --> 00:15:04.493
Yeah, this is interesting because this is these are Permian creatures, which is before the age of dinosaurs showed up, way before lizards showed up.

00:15:04.885 --> 00:15:07.806
This is a very ancient group of reptiles.

00:15:08.206 --> 00:15:21.950
So the fact that this trait had already evolved back then is really is really cool to see it showing up in a much earlier group of animals using this feature the same way.

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:29.273
Yeah, it's a very recently a very unique form of convergent evolution, which is cool.

00:15:29.328 --> 00:15:40.632
Now, one of the other interesting things they noticed, which I thought was very cool, is they actually noticed a difference between the juveniles and adults on the cracks in the tail.

00:15:41.153 --> 00:15:49.275
When they looked at the juveniles, the cracks were more pronounced and more substantial.

00:15:50.349 --> 00:15:58.452
While they looked at the adults, they seem to be a little more fused or less, less weak.

00:15:59.252 --> 00:15:59.852
Interesting.

00:16:00.333 --> 00:16:13.057
Which could suggest that as they grew, they lost the need to evade predators as much when small young cotylosaurs, they need this ability to escape.

00:16:13.147 --> 00:16:16.348
And then once they get bigger, they stop needing to run away as much.

00:16:17.089 --> 00:16:17.729
Interesting.

00:16:17.789 --> 00:16:18.309
Yeah.

00:16:18.829 --> 00:16:20.030
It's a very cool finding.

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:20.750
Mm hmm.

00:16:21.191 --> 00:16:22.231
It's Oh, it's incredible.

00:16:22.611 --> 00:16:28.294
Always, always incredible what sort of things we're able to figure out about the fossil creatures.

00:16:28.714 --> 00:16:28.954
Mm hmm.

00:16:29.284 --> 00:16:40.189
It's I like these things because they always remind me and hopefully us as a as a group that animals have been complex for a very long time.

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:41.210
Yes.

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:49.994
And they've been doing cool tactical behaviors to survive way longer than we often give them credit for.

00:16:50.794 --> 00:16:51.474
Absolutely.

00:16:52.066 --> 00:16:56.888
My second news piece has to do with two things that we don't talk very often about.

00:16:57.348 --> 00:17:02.511
One is modern creature news and the other is bugs.

00:17:02.891 --> 00:17:03.331
Gasp.

00:17:03.671 --> 00:17:12.435
This is a study about spiders in Hawaii and a very interesting trend in the way they have evolved that they've spread through the islands.

00:17:12.986 --> 00:17:17.268
So this is a study by Rosemary Gillespie et al in current biology.

00:17:17.788 --> 00:17:20.909
They were looking at a particular group of spiders called stick spiders.

00:17:22.090 --> 00:17:23.911
These are very slow moving.

00:17:23.931 --> 00:17:29.973
They have these long abdomens and their bodies are kind of long and stick like they were.

00:17:30.053 --> 00:17:35.676
They were the what the article that I read referred to them as the sloths of the spider world.

00:17:36.107 --> 00:17:38.488
Very slow, but they're predators like all spiders.

00:17:39.148 --> 00:17:43.770
And what's really interesting is that as you hop between the islands of Hawaii, the big four islands, right?

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:50.653
Kauai, Oahu, Molokai, Hawaii, you see over and over again three color variations in the spiders.

00:17:51.214 --> 00:17:51.434
Mm hmm.

00:17:51.914 --> 00:17:55.336
Gold, which camouflage against the underside of the leaves.

00:17:56.344 --> 00:18:02.247
Dark brown, which camouflage against rocks and bark and white, which camouflage against lichens.

00:18:02.828 --> 00:18:03.228
Cool.

00:18:03.568 --> 00:18:07.530
And this study was basically looking at how have they evolved, right?

00:18:07.550 --> 00:18:10.532
What are the relationships between these groups of spiders?

00:18:11.343 --> 00:18:21.687
And you might expect that a group of white spiders spread from island to island and a group of brown spiders spread and a group of gold spiders spread from island to island.

00:18:22.027 --> 00:18:23.067
But that's not what they found.

00:18:23.605 --> 00:18:37.570
What they found was that the spiders on each island were more closely related to each other than they were to the spiders on the other islands, which suggests that these spiders dispersed to the islands.

00:18:37.667 --> 00:19:00.236
And every time they went to the islands, they re-evolved gold, white and brown variants, which is really an interesting finding because that raises all sorts of questions such as why are you continuously evolving those same three colors?

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:03.951
What are the limitations there?

00:19:05.251 --> 00:19:08.352
There's a long standing question in evolution.

00:19:08.712 --> 00:19:11.693
If you rewound the tape, would you get the same stuff?

00:19:12.113 --> 00:19:12.333
Yeah.

00:19:12.613 --> 00:19:17.374
If you reset the earth to 60 million years ago and ran it again, would you get all the same creatures?

00:19:17.774 --> 00:19:18.134
Mm hmm.

00:19:18.494 --> 00:19:24.176
In this particular case, we're kind of seeing that the same things have evolved over and over again.

00:19:24.664 --> 00:19:25.864
Take that chaos theory.

00:19:28.125 --> 00:19:40.050
Now, this has not only been seen in these spiders, there's another group of spiders called the long jawed goblin spiders, which had a similar thing throughout the Hawaiian Islands.

00:19:40.846 --> 00:19:52.510
A similar thing has also been seen with anoles, which are lizards in the greater Antilles, where they've evolved certain types of lifestyles over and over again on each island.

00:19:52.810 --> 00:20:01.573
In this case, the authors suspect that there's two ways that they might explain this, and they don't have to be mutually exclusive.

00:20:03.106 --> 00:20:11.048
One is that these are the only easy ways for the spiders to live.

00:20:11.928 --> 00:20:22.171
That living on the bark, living on the lichens, living underneath the leaves, those are the three best ways to be a spider on a Hawaiian island, to be a twig spider.

00:20:22.751 --> 00:20:26.231
So they constantly fall into those niches over and over again.

00:20:26.725 --> 00:20:49.192
Another explanation might be that it is a physical limitation, and they described how the color variation is really just a change in certain molecules on the skin, or on the outer layer of the body, and how those molecules interact with the pigments.

00:20:50.167 --> 00:20:59.650
So it could be that they haven't come across a change that would allow for a different variation outside of those three color variations.

00:21:00.130 --> 00:21:13.753
But what's really interesting, the other of course thing that's really interesting, is that while this pattern has been seen in at least two groups of spiders and at least one group of lizards, there's tons of spiders and lizards that we don't see this with.

00:21:14.106 --> 00:21:36.971
So this is something that is happening to this group of spiders and a couple of other groups, which suggests that either based on their environment or based on their physical mutability, they are limited in what they are capable of doing in these niches, which is just one of the coolest things you could find.

00:21:37.031 --> 00:21:38.431
I think that's really, really fascinating.

00:21:38.828 --> 00:21:57.213
That's really cool. The thing I really like about that is the fact that you typically think that it's completely random mutations, but it's often easy to forget, or sometimes you come across times where it's like, yes, any mutation could happen, but this mutation is real easy.

00:21:58.188 --> 00:22:14.514
So it just keeps happening. And so it allows them to evolve in similar ways. I also really like the mental image of the spiders being on an island and be like, look at us, we're so clever. Those other spiders, they got no clue how to live.

00:22:16.075 --> 00:22:17.716
We came up with three different colors.

00:22:17.807 --> 00:22:22.009
Yeah, man, when we have our annual meeting, they're gonna be so impressed.

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:26.010
Oh, man.

00:22:28.831 --> 00:22:37.954
That's very cool. Now, my last one has a similar thread in that it deals with partially modern groups.

00:22:39.745 --> 00:22:47.127
And if you had to, if I forced everyone to make a guess as to what group it dealt with, you'd never get it.

00:22:49.408 --> 00:22:52.929
This is a report about alligators versus crocodiles.

00:22:53.449 --> 00:22:58.750
So moving on to our main discussion for the episode.

00:22:58.890 --> 00:23:01.351
This is important. This is important. You all need to hear this.

00:23:02.705 --> 00:23:21.612
This is a recent study that looked at limb measurement in specifically crocodiloids and alligatoroids, which would include alligators and caimans in their group and all crocs in theirs.

